{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20705","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20705","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.20705","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaocong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shaocong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kairui","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Kairui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Pei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenqi","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Wenqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minjing","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Minjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong-Jin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yong-Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EEGDM: Learning EEG Representation with Latent Diffusion Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T12:23:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T00:35:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-19T02:47:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-22T01:14:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-16T01:44:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-17T00:19:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-30T12:43:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:05:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-02T11:59:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-10-05T00:53:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/tbme.2026.3740193","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in self-supervised learning for EEG representation have largely relied on masked reconstruction, where models are trained to recover randomly masked signal segments. While effective at modeling local dependencies, the training objective of masked reconstruction does not compel the model to capture global generative constraints essential for characterizing neural activity. To address this limitation, we propose EEGDM, a novel self-supervised framework that leverages latent diffusion models to generate EEG signals as an objective. Unlike masked reconstruction, diffusion-based generation progressively denoises signals from noise to realism, compelling the model to capture holistic temporal patterns and cross-channel relationships. Specifically, EEGDM incorporates an EEG encoder that distills raw signals and their channel augmentations into a compact representation, which serves as conditional information to guide the diffusion denoising process, thereby enabling the encoder and diffusion model to be jointly optimized through the generative objective. This design endows EEGDM with a compact latent space, which not only offers ample control over the generative process but also can be leveraged for downstream tasks. Experimental results show that EEGDM (1) reconstructs high-quality EEG signals, (2) learns robust representations, and (3) achieves competitive performance across diverse downstream tasks, thus exploring a new direction for self-supervised EEG representation learning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper was accepted by IEEE Transactions on Biomedical Engineering"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.20705","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-08-29T01:38:45Z","registered":"2025-08-29T01:38:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-05T02:42:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21046","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21046","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21046","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renshan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Renshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liqiang","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Liqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CogVLA: Cognition-Aligned Vision-Language-Action Model via Instruction-Driven Routing \u0026amp; Sparsification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T17:50:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T00:51:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-01T15:48:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:03:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-27T08:39:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-28T00:48:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models built on pre-trained Vision-Language Models (VLMs) require extensive post-training, resulting in high computational overhead that limits scalability and deployment.We propose CogVLA, a Cognition-Aligned Vision-Language-Action framework that leverages instruction-driven routing and sparsification to improve both efficiency and performance. CogVLA draws inspiration from human multimodal coordination and introduces a 3-stage progressive architecture. 1) Encoder-FiLM based Aggregation Routing (EFA-Routing) injects instruction information into the vision encoder to selectively aggregate and compress dual-stream visual tokens, forming a instruction-aware latent representation. 2) Building upon this compact visual encoding, LLM-FiLM based Pruning Routing (LFP-Routing) introduces action intent into the language model by pruning instruction-irrelevant visually grounded tokens, thereby achieving token-level sparsity. 3) To ensure that compressed perception inputs can still support accurate and coherent action generation, we introduce V-L-A Coupled Attention (CAtten), which combines causal vision-language attention with bidirectional action parallel decoding. Extensive experiments on the LIBERO benchmark and real-world robotic tasks demonstrate that CogVLA achieves state-of-the-art performance with success rates of 97.4% and 70.0%, respectively, while reducing training costs by 2.5-fold and decreasing inference latency by 2.8-fold compared to OpenVLA. CogVLA is open-sourced and publicly available at https://github.com/JiuTian-VL/CogVLA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to NeurIPS 2025, Project Page: https://jiutian-vl.github.io/CogVLA-page"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21046","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-08-29T01:47:35Z","registered":"2025-08-29T01:47:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-28T02:52:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21613","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21613","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21613","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhibin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhe","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Junhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianyu","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Qianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rong","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Rong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengxi","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hengxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinjing","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xinjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanghuan","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Guanghuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiheng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zhiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongjin","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yongjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Chameleon: Adaptive Fault Tolerance for Distributed Training via Real-time Policy Selection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T13:22:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-01T00:35:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-14T09:15:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:39:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-22T02:45:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:09:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-20T07:16:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:46:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Training large language models faces frequent interruptions due to various faults, demanding robust fault-tolerance. Existing backup-free methods, such as redundant computation, dynamic parallelism, and data rerouting, each incur performance penalties, whether from ongoing overhead, lengthy reconfigurations, or post-recovery inefficiencies. We propose Chameleon, an adaptive fault-tolerant system that intelligently selects optimal recovery strategies when a failure occurs. Chameleon achieves this through a unified performance model, expedient execution plan search, accurate performance estimation, and efficient communication optimizations. Experiments on a 32-card cluster show that Chameleon maintains a performance gap of within 11.00% between post-recovery and failure-free training, while preserving model convergence and efficient memory usage. Compared to state-of-the-art methods, Chameleon achieves up to 1.229x and 1.355x higher average throughput than Oobleck and Recycle, respectively."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21613","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-01T01:31:25Z","registered":"2025-09-01T01:31:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:48:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20640","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20640","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.20640","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayan","familyName":"Banerjee","name":"Banerjee, Ayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fernando","familyName":"Vilariño","name":"Vilariño, Fernando","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josep","familyName":"Lladós","name":"Lladós, Josep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CraftGraffiti: Exploring Human Identity with Custom Graffiti Art via Facial-Preserving Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T10:38:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T00:30:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-11T07:27:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T00:26:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Preserving facial identity under extreme stylistic transformation remains a major challenge in generative art. In graffiti, a high-contrast, abstract medium, subtle distortions to the eyes, nose, or mouth can erase the subject's recognizability, undermining both personal and cultural authenticity. We present CraftGraffiti, an end-to-end text-guided graffiti generation framework designed with facial feature preservation as a primary objective. Given an input image and a style and pose descriptive prompt, CraftGraffiti first applies graffiti style transfer via LoRA-fine-tuned pretrained diffusion transformer, then enforces identity fidelity through a face-consistent self-attention mechanism that augments attention layers with explicit identity embeddings. Pose customization is achieved without keypoints, using CLIP-guided prompt extension to enable dynamic re-posing while retaining facial coherence. We formally justify and empirically validate the \"style-first, identity-after\" paradigm, showing it reduces attribute drift compared to the reverse order. Quantitative results demonstrate competitive facial feature consistency and state-of-the-art aesthetic and human preference scores, while qualitative analyses and a live deployment at the Cruilla Festival highlight the system's real-world creative impact. CraftGraffiti advances the goal of identity-respectful AI-assisted artistry, offering a principled approach for blending stylistic freedom with recognizability in creative AI applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.20640","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-08-29T01:37:09Z","registered":"2025-08-29T01:37:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:16:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21019","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21019","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21019","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxiang","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Jiaxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuhua","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Xuhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyi Henry","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Hongyi Henry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenyue","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianxiang","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Tianxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinglin","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Qinglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Phased One-Step Adversarial Equilibrium for Video Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T17:20:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T00:50:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-20T03:52:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-21T01:16:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-25T11:59:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-29T01:17:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video diffusion generation suffers from critical sampling efficiency bottlenecks, particularly for large-scale models and long contexts. Existing video acceleration methods, adapted from image-based techniques, lack a single-step distillation ability for large-scale video models and task generalization for conditional downstream tasks. To bridge this gap, we propose the Video Phased Adversarial Equilibrium (V-PAE), a distillation framework that enables high-quality, single-step video generation from large-scale video models. Our approach employs a two-phase process. (i) Stability priming is a warm-up process to align the distributions of real and generated videos. It improves the stability of single-step adversarial distillation in the following process. (ii) Unified adversarial equilibrium is a flexible self-adversarial process that reuses generator parameters for the discriminator backbone. It achieves a co-evolutionary adversarial equilibrium in the Gaussian noise space. For the conditional tasks, we primarily preserve video-image subject consistency, which is caused by semantic degradation and conditional frame collapse during the distillation training in image-to-video (I2V) generation. Comprehensive experiments on VBench-I2V demonstrate that V-PAE outperforms existing acceleration methods by an average of 5.8% in the overall quality score, including semantic alignment, temporal coherence, and frame quality. In addition, our approach reduces the diffusion latency of the large-scale video model (e.g., Wan2.1-I2V-14B) by 100 times, while preserving competitive performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by AAAI 2026. Project Page: https://v-pae.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21019","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-08-29T01:46:53Z","registered":"2025-08-29T01:46:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-29T02:42:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21058","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21058","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21058","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengqu","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Shengqu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ceyuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Ceyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lvmin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lvmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuwei","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junfei","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Junfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Ziyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinghao","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhenheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alan","familyName":"Yuille","name":"Yuille, Alan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leonidas","familyName":"Guibas","name":"Guibas, Leonidas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maneesh","familyName":"Agrawala","name":"Agrawala, Maneesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gordon","familyName":"Wetzstein","name":"Wetzstein, Gordon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mixture of Contexts for Long Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T17:57:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T00:52:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-05T08:28:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:51:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T10:22:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-10T01:37:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long video generation is fundamentally a long context memory problem: models must retain and retrieve salient events across a long range without collapsing or drifting. However, scaling diffusion transformers to generate long-context videos is fundamentally limited by the quadratic cost of self-attention, which makes memory and computation intractable and difficult to optimize for long sequences. We recast long-context video generation as an internal information retrieval task and propose a simple, learnable sparse attention routing module, Mixture of Contexts (MoC), as an effective long-term memory retrieval engine. In MoC, each query dynamically selects a few informative chunks plus mandatory anchors (caption, local windows) to attend to, with causal routing that prevents loop closures. As we scale the data and gradually sparsify the routing, the model allocates compute to salient history, preserving identities, actions, and scenes over minutes of content. Efficiency follows as a byproduct of retrieval (near-linear scaling), which enables practical training and synthesis, and the emergence of memory and consistency at the scale of minutes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://primecai.github.io/moc/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21058","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-08-29T01:47:53Z","registered":"2025-08-29T01:47:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-10T03:15:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20840","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20840","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.20840","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Qiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liujia","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Liujia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaixin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Kaixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongchao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yongchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiange","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiange","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Haoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yating","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yating","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"He","name":"He, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yilun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xili","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Xili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanyang","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Nanyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinying","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Qinying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Primitive Embodied World Models: Towards Scalable Robotic Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T14:31:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T00:42:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-20T03:42:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:16:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-24T03:42:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-25T02:14:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While video-generation-based embodied world models have gained increasing attention, their reliance on large-scale embodied interaction data remains a key bottleneck. The scarcity, difficulty of collection, and high dimensionality of embodied data fundamentally limit the alignment granularity between language and actions and exacerbate the challenge of long-horizon video generation--hindering generative models from achieving a \"GPT moment\" in the embodied domain. There is a naive observation: the diversity of embodied data far exceeds the relatively small space of possible primitive motions. Based on this insight, we propose a novel paradigm for world modeling--Primitive Embodied World Models (PEWM). By restricting video generation to fixed short horizons, our approach 1) enables fine-grained alignment between linguistic concepts and visual representations of robotic actions, 2) reduces learning complexity, 3) improves data efficiency in embodied data collection, and 4) decreases inference latency. By equipping with a modular Vision-Language Model (VLM) planner and a Start-Goal heatmap Guidance mechanism (SGG), PEWM further enables flexible closed-loop control and supports compositional generalization of primitive-level policies over extended, complex tasks. Our framework leverages the spatiotemporal vision priors in video models and the semantic awareness of VLMs to bridge the gap between fine-grained physical interaction and high-level reasoning, paving the way toward scalable, interpretable, and general-purpose embodied intelligence."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.20840","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-08-29T01:42:27Z","registered":"2025-08-29T01:42:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-25T04:48:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00310","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00310","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.00310","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuangge","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shuangge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tesca","familyName":"Fitzgerald","name":"Fitzgerald, Tesca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TReF-6: Inferring Task-Relevant Frames from a Single Demonstration for One-Shot Skill Generalization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-30T01:54:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:11:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-28T17:29:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-30T01:15:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robots often struggle to generalize from a single demonstration due to the lack of a transferable and interpretable spatial representation. In this work, we introduce TReF-6, a method that infers a simplified, abstracted 6DoF Task-Relevant Frame from a single trajectory. Our approach identifies an influence point purely from the trajectory geometry to define the origin for a local frame, which serves as a reference for parameterizing a Dynamic Movement Primitive (DMP). This influence point captures the task's spatial structure, extending the standard DMP formulation beyond start-goal imitation. The inferred frame is semantically grounded via a vision-language model and localized in novel scenes by Grounded-SAM, enabling functionally consistent skill generalization. We validate TReF-6 in simulation and demonstrate robustness to trajectory noise. We further deploy an end-to-end pipeline on real-world manipulation tasks, showing that TReF-6 supports one-shot imitation learning that preserves task intent across diverse object configurations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00310","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T03:29:36Z","registered":"2025-09-03T03:29:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-30T05:16:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04474","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04474","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04474","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengyin","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Shengyin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingzhao","familyName":"Lian","name":"Lian, Yingzhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weizhe","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Weizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui-Ling","familyName":"Zhen","name":"Zhen, Hui-Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianzhi","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xianzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxuan","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Mingxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scaling Up, Speeding Up: A Benchmark of Speculative Decoding for Efficient LLM Test-Time Scaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-30T01:54:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-08T00:00:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Test-time scaling has emerged as a powerful paradigm for enhancing the reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs) by allocating additional computational resources during inference. However, this paradigm is inherently inefficient due to the generation of redundant and repetitive reasoning traces, leading to significant computational overhead. Speculative decoding offers a promising avenue for mitigating this inefficiency, yet its efficacy in the structured, repetition-rich context of test-time scaling remains largely unexplored. To bridge this gap, we introduce the first comprehensive benchmark designed to evaluate speculative decoding methods for accelerating LLM test-time scaling. Our benchmark provides consistent experimental protocols across representative test-time scaling paradigms (e.g., Best-of-N sampling and multi-round thinking), enabling a fair comparison of three major categories of speculative decoding: model-based, training-based, and n-gram-based methods. Extensive experiments reveal that simple n-gram-based methods effectively capture repetitive patterns, demonstrating unique potential in accelerating test-time scaling. This phenomenon demonstrates the value of integrating n-gram-based methods with model-based or training-based approaches to balance acceleration for both repetitive and diverse reasoning in test-time scaling. We hope this benchmark spurs further research on speculative decoding for test-time scaling, enabling faster and more practical reasoning in LLMs through better handling of repetitive and diverse reasoning paths."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04474","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-08T01:25:49Z","registered":"2025-09-08T01:25:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-08T01:25:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00300","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00300","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.00300","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ghadeer","familyName":"Almusaddar","name":"Almusaddar, Ghadeer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yicheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saber","familyName":"Ganjisaffar","name":"Ganjisaffar, Saber","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Barry","familyName":"Williams","name":"Williams, Barry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu David","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dmitry","familyName":"Ponomarev","name":"Ponomarev, Dmitry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nael","familyName":"Abu-Ghazaleh","name":"Abu-Ghazaleh, Nael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ShadowScope: GPU Monitoring and Validation via Composable Side Channel Signals"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-30T01:38:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:11:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T20:21:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:06:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As modern systems increasingly rely on GPUs for computationally intensive tasks such as machine learning acceleration, ensuring the integrity of GPU computation has become critically important. Recent studies have shown that GPU kernels are vulnerable to both traditional memory safety issues (e.g., buffer overflow attacks) and emerging microarchitectural threats (e.g., Rowhammer attacks), many of which manifest as anomalous execution behaviors observable through side-channel signals. However, existing golden model based validation approaches that rely on such signals are fragile, highly sensitive to interference, and do not scale well across GPU workloads with diverse scheduling behaviors. To address these challenges, we propose ShadowScope, a monitoring and validation framework that leverages a composable golden model. Instead of building a single monolithic reference, ShadowScope decomposes trusted kernel execution into modular, repeatable functions that encode key behavioral features. This composable design captures execution patterns at finer granularity, enabling robust validation that is resilient to noise, workload variation, and interference across GPU workloads. To further reduce reliance on noisy software-only monitoring, we introduce ShadowScope+, a hardware-assisted validation mechanism that integrates lightweight on-chip checks into the GPU pipeline. ShadowScope+ achieves high validation accuracy with an average runtime overhead of just 4.6%, while incurring minimal hardware and design complexity. Together, these contributions demonstrate that side-channel observability can be systematically repurposed into a practical defense for GPU kernel integrity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00300","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T03:29:21Z","registered":"2025-09-03T03:29:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-05T01:58:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00336","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00336","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.00336","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"An B.","familyName":"Vuong","name":"Vuong, An B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael T.","familyName":"McCann","name":"McCann, Michael T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Javier E.","familyName":"Santos","name":"Santos, Javier E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yen Ting","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yen Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are We Really Learning the Score Function? Reinterpreting Diffusion Models Through Wasserstein Gradient Flow Matching"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-30T03:30:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:13:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models are commonly interpreted as learning the score function, i.e., the gradient of the log-density of noisy data. However, this assumption implies that the target of learning is a conservative vector field, which is not enforced by the neural network architectures used in practice. We present numerical evidence that trained diffusion networks violate both integral and differential constraints required of true score functions, demonstrating that the learned vector fields are not conservative. Despite this, the models perform remarkably well as generative mechanisms. To explain this apparent paradox, we advocate a new theoretical perspective: diffusion training is better understood as flow matching to the velocity field of a Wasserstein Gradient Flow (WGF), rather than as score learning for a reverse-time stochastic differential equation. Under this view, the \"probability flow\" arises naturally from the WGF framework, eliminating the need to invoke reverse-time SDE theory and clarifying why generative sampling remains successful even when the neural vector field is not a true score. We further show that non-conservative errors from neural approximation do not necessarily harm density transport. Our results advocate for adopting the WGF perspective as a principled, elegant, and theoretically grounded framework for understanding diffusion generative models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00336","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T03:30:14Z","registered":"2025-09-03T03:30:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-03T03:30:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00177","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00177","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.00177","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faizan Farooq","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Faizan Farooq","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vladan","familyName":"Stojnić","name":"Stojnić, Vladan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zakaria","familyName":"Laskar","name":"Laskar, Zakaria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed","familyName":"Elhoseiny","name":"Elhoseiny, Mohamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giorgos","familyName":"Tolias","name":"Tolias, Giorgos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Category-level Text-to-Image Retrieval Improved: Bridging the Domain Gap with Diffusion Models and Vision Encoders"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T18:24:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:04:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work explores text-to-image retrieval for queries that specify or describe a semantic category. While vision-and-language models (VLMs) like CLIP offer a straightforward open-vocabulary solution, they map text and images to distant regions in the representation space, limiting retrieval performance. To bridge this modality gap, we propose a two-step approach. First, we transform the text query into a visual query using a generative diffusion model. Then, we estimate image-to-image similarity with a vision model. Additionally, we introduce an aggregation network that combines multiple generated images into a single vector representation and fuses similarity scores across both query modalities. Our approach leverages advancements in vision encoders, VLMs, and text-to-image generation models. Extensive evaluations show that it consistently outperforms retrieval methods relying solely on text queries. Source code is available at: https://github.com/faixan-khan/cletir"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"BMVC 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00177","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T03:26:18Z","registered":"2025-09-03T03:26:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-03T03:26:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21727","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21727","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21727","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazheng","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Jiazheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hai","familyName":"Ci","name":"Ci, Hai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongbin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hongbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hangjie","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Hangjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mike Zheng","familyName":"Shou","name":"Shou, Mike Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OptMark: Robust Multi-bit Diffusion Watermarking via Inference Time Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T15:50:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-01T00:42:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Watermarking diffusion-generated images is crucial for copyright protection and user tracking. However, current diffusion watermarking methods face significant limitations: zero-bit watermarking systems lack the capacity for large-scale user tracking, while multi-bit methods are highly sensitive to certain image transformations or generative attacks, resulting in a lack of comprehensive robustness. In this paper, we propose OptMark, an optimization-based approach that embeds a robust multi-bit watermark into the intermediate latents of the diffusion denoising process. OptMark strategically inserts a structural watermark early to resist generative attacks and a detail watermark late to withstand image transformations, with tailored regularization terms to preserve image quality and ensure imperceptibility. To address the challenge of memory consumption growing linearly with the number of denoising steps during optimization, OptMark incorporates adjoint gradient methods, reducing memory usage from O(N) to O(1). Experimental results demonstrate that OptMark achieves invisible multi-bit watermarking while ensuring robust resilience against valuemetric transformations, geometric transformations, editing, and regeneration attacks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21727","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-01T01:34:37Z","registered":"2025-09-01T01:34:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-01T01:34:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21547","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21547","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21547","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jens","familyName":"Leysen","name":"Leysen, Jens","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Favier","name":"Favier, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bart","familyName":"Goethals","name":"Goethals, Bart","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"What Data is Really Necessary? A Feasibility Study of Inference Data Minimization for Recommender Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T12:01:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-01T00:32:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3746252.3761058","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Data minimization is a legal principle requiring personal data processing to be limited to what is necessary for a specified purpose. Operationalizing this principle for recommender systems, which rely on extensive personal data, remains a significant challenge. This paper conducts a feasibility study on minimizing implicit feedback inference data for such systems. We propose a novel problem formulation, analyze various minimization techniques, and investigate key factors influencing their effectiveness. We demonstrate that substantial inference data reduction is technically feasible without significant performance loss. However, its practicality is critically determined by two factors: the technical setting (e.g., performance targets, choice of model) and user characteristics (e.g., history size, preference complexity). Thus, while we establish its technical feasibility, we conclude that data minimization remains practically challenging and its dependence on the technical and user context makes a universal standard for data `necessity' difficult to implement."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication at the 34th ACM International Conference on Information and Knowledge Management (CIKM '25), November 10-14, 2025, Seoul, Republic of Korea"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21547","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-01T01:29:47Z","registered":"2025-09-01T01:29:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-01T01:29:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21542","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21542","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21542","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziwei","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Ziwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed","familyName":"Sayed","name":"Sayed, Mohamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steven L.","familyName":"Waslander","name":"Waslander, Steven L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sara","familyName":"Vicente","name":"Vicente, Sara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniyar","familyName":"Turmukhambetov","name":"Turmukhambetov, Daniyar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Firman","name":"Firman, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Complete Gaussian Splats from a Single Image with Denoising Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T11:55:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-01T00:31:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Gaussian splatting typically requires dense observations of the scene and can fail to reconstruct occluded and unobserved areas. We propose a latent diffusion model to reconstruct a complete 3D scene with Gaussian splats, including the occluded parts, from only a single image during inference. Completing the unobserved surfaces of a scene is challenging due to the ambiguity of the plausible surfaces. Conventional methods use a regression-based formulation to predict a single \"mode\" for occluded and out-of-frustum surfaces, leading to blurriness, implausibility, and failure to capture multiple possible explanations. Thus, they often address this problem partially, focusing either on objects isolated from the background, reconstructing only visible surfaces, or failing to extrapolate far from the input views. In contrast, we propose a generative formulation to learn a distribution of 3D representations of Gaussian splats conditioned on a single input image. To address the lack of ground-truth training data, we propose a Variational AutoReconstructor to learn a latent space only from 2D images in a self-supervised manner, over which a diffusion model is trained. Our method generates faithful reconstructions and diverse samples with the ability to complete the occluded surfaces for high-quality 360-degree renderings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Main paper: 11 pages; Supplementary materials: 7 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21542","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-01T01:29:40Z","registered":"2025-09-01T01:29:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-01T01:29:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21330","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21330","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21330","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuhan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaohui","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Zhaohui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaohui","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Yaohui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yining","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stage-Diff: Stage-wise Long-Term Time Series Generation Based on Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T05:10:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-01T00:14:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative models have been successfully used in the field of time series generation. However, when dealing with long-term time series, which span over extended periods and exhibit more complex long-term temporal patterns, the task of generation becomes significantly more challenging. Long-term time series exhibit long-range temporal dependencies, but their data distribution also undergoes gradual changes over time. Finding a balance between these long-term dependencies and the drift in data distribution is a key challenge. On the other hand, long-term time series contain more complex interrelationships between different feature sequences, making the task of effectively capturing both intra-sequence and inter-sequence dependencies another important challenge. To address these issues, we propose Stage-Diff, a staged generative model for long-term time series based on diffusion models. First, through stage-wise sequence generation and inter-stage information transfer, the model preserves long-term sequence dependencies while enabling the modeling of data distribution shifts. Second, within each stage, progressive sequence decomposition is applied to perform channel-independent modeling at different time scales, while inter-stage information transfer utilizes multi-channel fusion modeling. This approach combines the robustness of channel-independent modeling with the information fusion advantages of multi-channel modeling, effectively balancing the intra-sequence and inter-sequence dependencies of long-term time series. Extensive experiments on multiple real-world datasets validate the effectiveness of Stage-Diff in long-term time series generation tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21330","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-01T01:24:31Z","registered":"2025-09-01T01:24:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-01T01:24:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21230","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21230","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21230","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Curless","name":"Curless, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Gowanlock","name":"Gowanlock, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fast and Scalable Mixed Precision Euclidean Distance Calculations Using GPU Tensor Cores"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T21:46:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-01T00:07:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3754598.3754636","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern GPUs are equipped with tensor cores (TCs) that are commonly used for matrix multiplication in artificial intelligence workloads. However, because they have high computational throughput, they can lead to significant performance gains in other algorithms if they can be successfully exploited. We examine using TCs to compute Euclidean distance calculations, which are used in many data analytics applications. Prior work has only investigated using 64 bit floating point (FP64) data for computation; however, TCs can operate on lower precision floating point data (i.e., 16 bit matrix multiplication and 32 bit accumulation), which we refer to as FP16-32. FP16-32 TC peak throughput is so high that TCs are easily starved of data. We propose a Fast and Scalable Tensor core Euclidean Distance (FaSTED) algorithm. To achieve high computational throughput, we design FaSTED for significant hierarchical reuse of data and maximize memory utilization at every level (global memory, shared memory, and registers). We apply FaSTED to the application of similarity searches, which typically employ an indexing data structure to eliminate superfluous Euclidean distance calculations. We compare to the state-of-the-art (SOTA) TC Euclidean distance algorithm in the literature that employs FP64, as well as to two single precision (FP32) CUDA core algorithms that both employ an index. We find that across four real-world high-dimensional datasets spanning 128-960 dimensions, the mixed-precision brute force approach achieves a speedup over the SOTA algorithms of 2.5-51x. We also quantify the accuracy loss of our mixed precision algorithm to be less than \u0026lt;0.06% when compared to the FP64 baseline."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in the proceedings of the International Conference on Parallel Processing 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21230","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-01T01:22:07Z","registered":"2025-09-01T01:22:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-01T01:22:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21016","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21016","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21016","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luozhijie","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Luozhijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijie","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Zijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijie","familyName":"Diao","name":"Diao, Zijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lifeng","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Lifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Lamb","name":"Lamb, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xipeng","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Xipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Inference-Time Alignment Control for Diffusion Models with Reinforcement Learning Guidance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T17:18:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T00:50:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Denoising-based generative models, particularly diffusion and flow matching algorithms, have achieved remarkable success. However, aligning their output distributions with complex downstream objectives, such as human preferences, compositional accuracy, or data compressibility, remains challenging. While reinforcement learning (RL) fine-tuning methods, inspired by advances in RL from human feedback (RLHF) for large language models, have been adapted to these generative frameworks, current RL approaches are suboptimal for diffusion models and offer limited flexibility in controlling alignment strength after fine-tuning. In this work, we reinterpret RL fine-tuning for diffusion models through the lens of stochastic differential equations and implicit reward conditioning. We introduce Reinforcement Learning Guidance (RLG), an inference-time method that adapts Classifier-Free Guidance (CFG) by combining the outputs of the base and RL fine-tuned models via a geometric average. Our theoretical analysis shows that RLG's guidance scale is mathematically equivalent to adjusting the KL-regularization coefficient in standard RL objectives, enabling dynamic control over the alignment-quality trade-off without further training. Extensive experiments demonstrate that RLG consistently improves the performance of RL fine-tuned models across various architectures, RL algorithms, and downstream tasks, including human preferences, compositional control, compressibility, and text rendering. Furthermore, RLG supports both interpolation and extrapolation, thereby offering unprecedented flexibility in controlling generative alignment. Our approach provides a practical and theoretically sound solution for enhancing and controlling diffusion model alignment at inference. The source code for RLG is publicly available at the Github: https://github.com/jinluo12345/Reinforcement-learning-guidance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21016","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-08-29T01:46:49Z","registered":"2025-08-29T01:46:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-08-29T01:46:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20783","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20783","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.20783","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beth","familyName":"Pearson","name":"Pearson, Beth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bilal","familyName":"Boulbarss","name":"Boulbarss, Bilal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Wray","name":"Wray, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martha","familyName":"Lewis","name":"Lewis, Martha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Compositional Generalisation in VLMs and Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T13:45:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T00:39:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A fundamental aspect of the semantics of natural language is that novel meanings can be formed from the composition of previously known parts. Vision-language models (VLMs) have made significant progress in recent years, however, there is evidence that they are unable to perform this kind of composition. For example, given an image of a red cube and a blue cylinder, a VLM such as CLIP is likely to incorrectly label the image as a red cylinder or a blue cube, indicating it represents the image as a `bag-of-words' and fails to capture compositional semantics. Diffusion models have recently gained significant attention for their impressive generative abilities, and zero-shot classifiers based on diffusion models have been shown to perform competitively with CLIP in certain compositional tasks. In this work we explore whether the generative Diffusion Classifier has improved compositional generalisation abilities compared to discriminative models. We assess three models -- Diffusion Classifier, CLIP, and ViLT -- on their ability to bind objects with attributes and relations in both zero-shot learning (ZSL) and generalised zero-shot learning (GZSL) settings. Our results show that the Diffusion Classifier and ViLT perform well at concept binding tasks, but that all models struggle significantly with the relational GZSL task, underscoring the broader challenges VLMs face with relational reasoning. Analysis of CLIP embeddings suggests that the difficulty may stem from overly similar representations of relational concepts such as left and right. Code and dataset are available at: https://github.com/otmive/diffusion_classifier_clip"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages including references, 6 figures. Accepted at IWCS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.20783","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-08-29T01:40:48Z","registered":"2025-08-29T01:40:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-08-29T01:40:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20613","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20613","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.20613","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixiang","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Yixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanhan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yanhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Hongyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shu-Tao","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Shu-Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Revisiting the Privacy Risks of Split Inference: A GAN-Based Data Reconstruction Attack via Progressive Feature Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T10:00:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T00:29:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The growing complexity of Deep Neural Networks (DNNs) has led to the adoption of Split Inference (SI), a collaborative paradigm that partitions computation between edge devices and the cloud to reduce latency and protect user privacy. However, recent advances in Data Reconstruction Attacks (DRAs) reveal that intermediate features exchanged in SI can be exploited to recover sensitive input data, posing significant privacy risks. Existing DRAs are typically effective only on shallow models and fail to fully leverage semantic priors, limiting their reconstruction quality and generalizability across datasets and model architectures. In this paper, we propose a novel GAN-based DRA framework with Progressive Feature Optimization (PFO), which decomposes the generator into hierarchical blocks and incrementally refines intermediate representations to enhance the semantic fidelity of reconstructed images. To stabilize the optimization and improve image realism, we introduce an L1-ball constraint during reconstruction. Extensive experiments show that our method outperforms prior attacks by a large margin, especially in high-resolution scenarios, out-of-distribution settings, and against deeper and more complex DNNs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.20613","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-08-29T01:36:30Z","registered":"2025-08-29T01:36:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-08-29T01:36:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20586","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20586","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.20586","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Chong","name":"Chong, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanwei","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Yanwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shiyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuandi","familyName":"He","name":"He, Zhuandi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xujie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiling","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yiling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongmei","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Dongmei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodan","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Xiaodan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FastFit: Accelerating Multi-Reference Virtual Try-On via Cacheable Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.4.9","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T42 (Primary) 168T45 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T09:25:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T00:27:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite its great potential, virtual try-on technology is hindered from real-world application by two major challenges: the inability of current methods to support multi-reference outfit compositions (including garments and accessories), and their significant inefficiency caused by the redundant re-computation of reference features in each denoising step. To address these challenges, we propose FastFit, a high-speed multi-reference virtual try-on framework based on a novel cacheable diffusion architecture. By employing a Semi-Attention mechanism and substituting traditional timestep embeddings with class embeddings for reference items, our model fully decouples reference feature encoding from the denoising process with negligible parameter overhead. This allows reference features to be computed only once and losslessly reused across all steps, fundamentally breaking the efficiency bottleneck and achieving an average 3.5x speedup over comparable methods. Furthermore, to facilitate research on complex, multi-reference virtual try-on, we introduce DressCode-MR, a new large-scale dataset. It comprises 28,179 sets of high-quality, paired images covering five key categories (tops, bottoms, dresses, shoes, and bags), constructed through a pipeline of expert models and human feedback refinement. Extensive experiments on the VITON-HD, DressCode, and our DressCode-MR datasets show that FastFit surpasses state-of-the-art methods on key fidelity metrics while offering its significant advantage in inference efficiency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 10 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.20586","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-08-29T01:35:50Z","registered":"2025-08-29T01:35:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-08-29T01:35:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20471","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20471","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.20471","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiusi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiusi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jackson","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Jackson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyu","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Jinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miao","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Miao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuopu","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Tuopu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peijin","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Peijin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengxiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shengxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunlei","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Chunlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maolin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Maolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuzhan","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yuzhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengmeng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Mengmeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diange","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Diange","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Realistic and Controllable 3D Gaussian-Guided Object Editing for Driving Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T06:39:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T00:20:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Corner cases are crucial for training and validating autonomous driving systems, yet collecting them from the real world is often costly and hazardous. Editing objects within captured sensor data offers an effective alternative for generating diverse scenarios, commonly achieved through 3D Gaussian Splatting or image generative models. However, these approaches often suffer from limited visual fidelity or imprecise pose control. To address these issues, we propose G^2Editor, a framework designed for photorealistic and precise object editing in driving videos. Our method leverages a 3D Gaussian representation of the edited object as a dense prior, injected into the denoising process to ensure accurate pose control and spatial consistency. A scene-level 3D bounding box layout is employed to reconstruct occluded areas of non-target objects. Furthermore, to guide the appearance details of the edited object, we incorporate hierarchical fine-grained features as additional conditions during generation. Experiments on the Waymo Open Dataset demonstrate that G^2Editor effectively supports object repositioning, insertion, and deletion within a unified framework, outperforming existing methods in both pose controllability and visual quality, while also benefiting downstream data-driven tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.20471","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-08-29T01:32:59Z","registered":"2025-08-29T01:33:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-08-29T01:33:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":22,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=(doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.20613%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21016%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21230%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21547%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21727%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04474%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.00300%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.00310%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21613%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.20471%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.20586%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.20783%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21330%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21542%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.00177%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.20840%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21058%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21019%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.20640%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21046%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.20705%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.00336)\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=30"}}